{
  "concept": "BUILD-MAX-HEAP turns an arbitrary array into a max-heap by calling MAX-HEAPIFY on every internal node from ⌊n/2⌋ down to 1, each call sifting one value down until parents dominate their children.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal array bar labeled 'A' with ten cells holding 4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7 (array indices 1..10 implied by cell order)",
    "Rooted binary tree drawn from the same array: node 1 root, children 2i and 2i+1, ten nodes total, three levels plus one node on level four",
    "Circle node for each array slot, filled light gray by default, with the key value centered inside",
    "Small index numeral (1..10) placed outside and above-left/above-right of each node circle",
    "Straight edges from every parent to its left and right child",
    "Dark gray fill on the single node currently being processed (the root of the subtree passed to MAX-HEAPIFY)",
    "Italic label 'i' placed beside the dark gray node",
    "Comparison arrows / highlight from the current node i down to its left and right children during a compare step",
    "Swap highlight: the two circles exchanged in a swap are outlined and their values cross over",
    "Synchronized highlighting of the corresponding cell(s) in the array bar whenever a tree node is read or swapped",
    "Panel caption text (a)–(f) equivalent shown as a status line naming the current call, e.g. 'MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 5)'",
    "Legend: light gray = unmodified node, dark gray = current node i",
    "Status readout showing heap-size n = 10 and the current loop index i",
    "Success banner when every parent ≥ both children (final heap rooted at 16)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "heapifyEditor",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That MAX-HEAPIFY is a single downward path of compare-and-swap steps, and that BUILD-MAX-HEAP is just that routine invoked bottom-up; writing it wrong (comparing against the parent instead of the running largest, or dropping the recursive call) makes the violation visibly survive at the exact node where the logic broke.",
      "label": "Implement MAX-HEAPIFY, then run BUILD-MAX-HEAP",
      "entry_point": "maxHeapify(i, n)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "value(i)", "does": "read the key stored at index i (1-based)" },
        { "op": "left(i)", "does": "return index 2i" },
        { "op": "right(i)", "does": "return index 2i + 1" },
        { "op": "swap(i, j)", "does": "exchange the keys at indices i and j — the ONLY way to mutate the heap" },
        { "op": "mark(i, note)", "does": "shade node i dark gray as the current node and show note as the caption" },
        { "op": "compare(i, j)", "does": "draw a comparison arrow between i and j and return true when value(i) > value(j)" }
      ],
      "frame": "the full 10-node tree and the array bar, with the current index i shaded dark gray, the 1-2 child indices touched by the op arrowed or outlined, and the caption naming the op",
      "input": { "label": "Initial array A", "default": "4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7" },
      "sample_code": "function maxHeapify(i, n) {\n  mark(i, 'MAX-HEAPIFY at ' + i);\n  var l = left(i), r = right(i);\n  var largest = i;\n  if (l <= n && compare(l, largest)) largest = l;\n  if (r <= n && compare(r, largest)) largest = r;\n  if (largest !== i) {\n    swap(i, largest);\n    maxHeapify(largest, n);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function maxHeapify(i, n) {\n  mark(i, 'MAX-HEAPIFY at ' + i);\n  var l = left(i), r = right(i);\n  var largest = i;\n  if (l <= n && compare(l, i)) largest = l;\n  if (r <= n && compare(r, i)) largest = r;  // BUG: compares each child to i, not to the running largest\n  if (largest !== i) {\n    swap(i, largest);\n    // BUG: no recursive call — the displaced value never keeps sifting down\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "after BUILD-MAX-HEAP runs for i = 5 down to 1, every index i ≤ 5 satisfies value(i) >= value(2i) and value(i) >= value(2i+1), giving root 16"
    },
    {
      "id": "buildStep",
      "type": "state",
      "teaches": "The bottom-up loop order: heapification starts at the last internal node ⌊n/2⌋ = 5 and walks up to the root, so every call can assume both subtrees below it are already heaps.",
      "label": "BUILD-MAX-HEAP loop position",
      "states": ["i=5 (a)", "i=4 (b)", "i=3 (c)", "i=2 (d)", "i=1 (e)", "done (f)"],
      "default": "i=5 (a)"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The array is already a tree",
      "narration": "A = ⟨4, 1, 3, 2, 16, 9, 10, 14, 8, 7⟩ is drawn as a binary tree: index i has children 2i and 2i+1. Nothing has moved yet, and many parents are smaller than their children.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "i=5 (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Start at the last internal node, i = 5",
      "narration": "Leaves 6..10 are trivially heaps, so the first real work is at i = ⌊10/2⌋ = 5. Node 16 already dominates its only child 7, so the call changes nothing.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "i=5 (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "i = 4: the first swap",
      "narration": "Node 2 is smaller than both children 14 and 8; MAX-HEAPIFY picks the larger child 14 and swaps, sending 2 down to index 8.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "i=4 (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "i = 3 and i = 2: fix higher subtrees",
      "narration": "3 trades with 10, then 1 sifts down through 16 and then 7 — each call may travel several levels because the value must keep falling until it finds its place.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "i=3 (c)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "i = 1: the root sifts all the way down",
      "narration": "The root 4 is exchanged with 16, then continues down past 14 and 8 — the longest path of the whole build, and the reason the loop must end, not begin, at the root.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "i=1 (e)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A completed max-heap",
      "narration": "No dark node remains: root 16 is the maximum and every parent is ≥ both children. Compare your code's final tree with this one to confirm the property holds everywhere.",
      "state": { "buildStep": "done (f)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "MAX-HEAPIFY only fixes a violation at the top of a subtree whose children are already heaps — which is exactly why BUILD-MAX-HEAP must run bottom-up from ⌊n/2⌋ to 1, and why the displaced value has to keep sifting down recursively rather than stopping after one swap.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Top of the canvas: a horizontal array bar labeled italic 'A' with ten equal boxed cells, thin black borders, light gray fill, values 4 1 3 2 16 9 10 14 8 7. Below it, one large binary tree (the interactive replacement for the six static panels) laid out like the textbook: root at top center, level 2 at left/right, level 3 with four nodes, and a single level-4 node hanging under index 5. Nodes are circles with black outlines and light gray fill; the key value is centered in black; the array index is a smaller numeral just outside the circle (upper-left for left children, upper-right for right children). Edges are plain thin straight lines. The node currently passed to MAX-HEAPIFY is filled dark gray with white-on-gray legible text and an italic 'i' placed just outside it. During a compare op, draw thin arrows from i to the child/children being tested; during a swap, outline both circles in a strong accent color and animate the values exchanging. Mirror every touched index in the array bar with the same highlight so learners see array and tree as one object. A caption line under the tree names the current call, e.g. 'MAX-HEAPIFY(A, 4)', matching the (a)–(f) panel captions. Include a small legend (light gray = settled, dark gray = current i) and a status line with n = 10. Code editor sits beside or below the figure with Run / Step / Reset controls and a 'Run BUILD-MAX-HEAP' button that loops i = 5 down to 1 calling the learner's function; on completion show a green check if every parent ≥ its children, otherwise flag the first offending parent-child pair in red."
}

